/** \page xmlrpc XML-RPC Communication

@section xmlrpc_Intro Introduction

The XmlRpc module of the Pt framework support both, the client and server
side of XML-RPC. Blocking and non-blocking APIs exist to implement and
call XML-RPC service procedures. The XmlRpc module builds on top of the
Http module and uses the HTTP client and server implementation thereof.
Custom data-types can be used in XML-RPC procedures if they are serializable
using Pt's serialization API.


@section xmlrpc_Service Implementation of XML-RPC services

The XML-RPC server side API is implemented as a service for the Pt HTTP
server. Regular C++ functions or methods can be registered as XML-RPC 
procedures with a Pt::XmlRpc::Service. XML-RPC requests are processed
and dispatched to the respective procedure. The registered function or
method will potentially be called from one of the HTTP server threads.


@subsection xmlrpc_Service_1 Adding service class methods to a XML-RPC service

The following example shows a typical XML-RPC service, where member functions
are registered as XML-RPC procedures:

@code
class CalculatorService : public Pt::XmlRpc::Service
{
  public:
    CalculatorService()
    {
      registerMethod("add", *this, &CalculatorService::add);
      registerMethod("sub", *this, &CalculatorService::sub);
      registerMethod("multiply", *this, &CalculatorService::multiply);
      registerMethod("div", *this, &CalculatorService::div);
    }

    int add(int a, int b)
    { return a+b; }

    int sub(int a, int b)
    { return a-b; }

    int multiply(int a, int b)
    { return a*b; }

    int div(int a, int b)
    { return a/b; }
};
@endcode

The service needs to inherit Pt::XmlRpc::Service and register the XML-RPC
procedures in the constructor. The string passed to registerMethod() is
the name of the procedure and all XML-RPC requests with this 'methodName' 
XML tag will be dispatched to that method.


@subsection xmlrpc_Client_2 Non-Blocking XML-RPC service procedures

Since the server has only a limited number of worker threads, the XML-RPC
procedures should not block, for example to perform long I/O operations,
otherwise the performance of the HTTP server will be degraded.
Instead, the asynchronous API allows using a Pt::System::EventLoop from
within a XML-RPC procedure, as shown in the next example:

@code
class AsyncEcho : public Pt::XmlRpc::AsyncCall<std::string, std::string>
                , public Pt::Connectable
{
  public:   
    AsyncEcho(Pt::XmlRpc::ServiceProcedure& proc)
    : Pt::XmlRpc::AsyncCall<std::string, std::string>(proc)
    {
      _timer.timeout() += Pt::slot(*this, &AsyncEcho::onTimeout);
    }

  protected:
    virtual void onBeginCall(const std::string& msg)
    {
      _r = msg;

      _timer.setActive( this->loop() );
      _timer.start(1000);         
    }

    virtual const std::string& onEndCall()
    {
      return _r;
    }

    void onTimeout()
    {
      _timer.stop();
      _timer.detach();
      this->setReady();
    }

    private:
      std::string _r;
      Pt::System::Timer _timer;
};

class MyService : public Pt::XmlRpc::Service
{
  public:
    MyService()
    {
      registerAsyncMethod("async", *this, &CalculatorService::async);
    }

    AsyncEcho* asyncEcho(Pt::XmlRpc::ServiceProcedure& proc)
    {
      return new AsyncEcho(proc);
    }
};
@endcode

A member function is registered as a XML-RPC procedure in the constructor of
the service, but the registered member function is only a factory method for
an asynchronous XML-RPC call. The class AsyncEcho is an example of such an
asynchronous call and it needs to derive from Pt::XmlRpc::AsyncCall, where the 
template parameters describe the signature of the XML-RPC procedure. The first
parameter is the return type and the following parameters are the argument
types. In this case, the XML-RPC procedure returns a std::string and takes one
argument, also a std::string, when invoked. Two virtual functions have to be
implemented as part of the Pt::XmlRpc::AsyncCall interface. When the procedure
is invoked, onBeginCall() will be called and the arguments, as defined by the 
template parameters of AsyncCall, are passed. In the example, we use the event 
loop inherited from Pt::XmlRpc::AsyncCall, to start a timer. This is also the 
event loop of the server thread from which the procedure was invoked. When the 
timer runs out, AsyncCall::setReady() is called in the timer callback, to
notify the XML-RPC service that the asynchronous call has finished and finally
onEndCall() will be called to obtain the return value.


@subsection xmlrpc_Service_2 Using User-Defined Types in XML-RPC procedures

@code
struct Address
{
  std::string country;
  std::string city;
  std::string street;
  int code;
};

void operator>>=(const Pt::SerializationInfo& si, Address& address);
void operator<<=(Pt::SerializationInfo& si, const Address& address);

class MyService : public Pt::xmlrpc::Service
{
  public:
    MyService()
    {
      registerMethod("getPosition", *this, &MyService::getPosition);
    }

    Address getPosition();
};
@endcode

@code
void operator>>=(const Pt::SerializationInfo& si, Address& address)
{
  si.getMember("year")   >>= address.country;
  si.getMember("city")   >>= address.city;
  si.getMember("street") >>= address.street;
  si.getMember("code")   >>= address.code;
}

void operator<<=(Pt::SerializationInfo& si, const Address& address)
{
  si.addMember("country") <<= address.country;
  si.addMember("city")    <<= address.city;
  si.addMember("street")  <<= address.street;
  si.addMember("code")    <<= address.code;
  si.setTypeName("Address");
}
@endcode

@subsection xmlrpc_Service_3 Adding XML-RPC services to a HTTP Server

@code
int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
  Pt::System::MainLoop loop;

  // constructor takes address and port for listen
  Pt::Http::HttpServer server(loop, "127.0.0.1", 7002);

  // add the service to the server
  CalculatorService calc;
  Pt::Http::MapUrl mapurl("/Calc", calc);
  server.addServlet(mapurl);

  // begin accepting connections
  loop.run();
  return 0;
};
@endcode


@section xmlrpc_Client Implementation of a XML-RPC clients

@subsection xmlrpc_Client_1 Blocking calls to a XML-RPC service

@code
int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
  try
  {
    Pt::XmlRpc::HttpClient client("127.0.0.1", 7002, "/Calc");

    Pt::XmlRpc::RemoteProcedure<int, int, int> multiply(client, "multiply");
    int r = multiply(6, 7);

    std::cout << "Result: " << r << std::endl;
    return 0;
  }
  catch(std::exception& ex)  // may be Pt::xmlrpc::Fault
  {
    std::cerr << "Error: " << ex.what() << std::endl;
  }

  return 1
};
@endcode


@subsection xmlrpc_Client_2 Non-Blocking calls to a XML-RPC procedures

@code

void onResult(const Pt::XmlRpc::Result<int>& r)
{
  try
  {
    std::cout << "Result: " << r.get() << std::endl;
  }
  catch(const std::exception& ex)
  {
    std::cerr << "Error: " << ex.what() << std::endl;
  }
}

int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
  try
  {
    Pt::System::MainLoop loop;
    Pt::XmlRpc::HttpClient client(loop, "127.0.0.1", 7002, "/Calc");

    Pt::XmlRpc::RemoteProcedure<int, int, int> multiply(client, "multiply");
    multiply.finished += Pt::slot(&onResult);
    multiply.begin(6, 7);

    loop.run();
  }
  catch(const std::exception& ex)
  {
    std::cerr << "Error: " << ex.what() << std::endl;
    return 1;
  }

  return 0;
};

@endcode

@section xmlrpc_Protocol Protocol Details

XML-RPC is a Remote Procedure Calling protocol that works over the network.

An XML-RPC message is an HTTP-POST request. The body of the request is in
XML. A procedure executes on the server and the value it returns is also
formatted in XML.

Procedure parameters can be scalars, numbers, strings, dates, etc.; and
can also be complex record and list structures.

<b>Firewalls:</b> The goal of this protocol is to lay a compatible
foundation across different environments, no new power is provided beyond
the capabilities of the CGI interface. Firewall software can watch for POSTs
whose Content-Type is text/xml.

<b>Discoverability:</b> We wanted a clean, extensible format that's very
simple. It should be possible for an HTML coder to be able to look at a
file containing an XML-RPC procedure call, understand what it's doing, and
be able to modify it and have it work on the first or second try.

<b>Easy to implement:</b> We also wanted it to be an easy to implement
protocol that could quickly be adapted to run in other environments or
on other operating systems.


@subsection xmlrpc_Request_Example Request Example

Here's an example of an XML-RPC request:

@verbatim
POST /Calc HTTP/1.0
User-Agent: Frontier/5.1.2 (WinNT)
Host: betty.userland.com
Content-Type: text/xml
Content-length: 181

<?xml version="1.0"?>
<methodCall>
  <methodName>multiply</methodName>
  <params>
    <param> <value><int>6</int></value> </param>
    <param> <value><int>7</int></value> </param>
  </params>
</methodCall>
@endverbatim

The format of the URI in the first line of the header is not specified. For
example, it could be empty, a single slash, if the server is only handling
XML-RPC calls. However, if the server is handling a mix of incoming HTTP
requests, we allow the URI to help route the request to the code that handles
XML-RPC requests. In the example, the URI is /Calc, telling the server to
route the request to the "Calc" service.

The payload is in XML, a single <methodCall> structure.

The \<methodCall\> must contain a \<methodName\> sub-item, a string, containing
the name of the method to be called. The string may only contain identifier
characters, upper and lower-case A-Z, the numeric characters, 0-9, underscore,
dot, colon and slash. It's entirely up to the server to decide how to interpret
the characters in a methodName.

For example, the methodName could be the name of a file containing a script
that executes on an incoming request. It could be the name of a cell in a
database table. Or it could be a path to a file contained within a hierarchy
of folders and files.

If the procedure call has parameters, the \<methodCall\> must contain a
<params\> sub-item. The \<params\> sub-item can contain any number of
\<param\>s, each of which has a \<value\>.


@subsection xmlrpc_Response_Example Response Example

Here's an example of an XML-RPC response:

@verbatim
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Connection: close
Content-Length: 158
Content-Type: text/xml
Date: Fri, 17 Jul 2008 19:55:08 GMT
Server: UserLand Frontier/5.1.2-WinNT

<?xml version="1.0"?>
<methodResponse>
  <params>
    <param>
      <value><int>42</int></value>
    </param>
  </params>
</methodResponse>
@endverbatim

Unless there's a lower-level error, always return 200 OK.

The Content-Type is text/xml. Content-Length must be present and correct.

The body of the response is a single XML structure, a \<methodResponse\>,
which can contain a single \<params\> which contains a single <param> which
contains a single \<value\>.


@subsection xmlrpc_Fault_Example Fault Example

Here's an example of an XML-RPC fault response:

@verbatim
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Connection: close
Content-Length: 426
Content-Type: text/xml
Date: Fri, 17 Jul 1998 19:55:02 GMT
Server: UserLand Frontier/5.1.2-WinNT

<?xml version="1.0"?>
<methodResponse>
  <fault>
    <value>
      <struct>
        <member>
          <name>faultCode</name>
          <value><int>4</int></value>
        </member>
        <member>
          <name>faultString</name>
          <value><string>Too many parameters.</string></value>
        </member>
      </struct>
    </value>
  </fault>
</methodResponse>
@endverbatim

The \<methodResponse\> could also contain a \<fault\> which contains a
\<value\> which is a \<struct\> containing two elements, one named
\<faultCode\>, an <int> and one named \<faultString\>, a \<string\>.

A <methodResponse> can not contain both a <fault> and a <params>.


